**Question 7 (algorithm analysis, 10 points)**

Let n = a.size(), let d be the number of **distinct** values in a, and assume 1 <= k <= d. The function below returns the k most frequent values.

```cpp
std::vector<int> topK(const std::vector<int>& a, int k) {
    std::unordered_map<int, int> cnt;
    for (int x : a)
        ++cnt[x];                                                      // step 1
    using P = std::pair<int, int>;                                     // (count, value)
    std::priority_queue<P, std::vector<P>, std::greater<P>> pq;        // min-heap
    for (const auto& kv : cnt) {                                       // step 2
        pq.push({kv.second, kv.first});
        if ((int)pq.size() > k)
            pq.pop();
    }
    std::vector<int> res;
    while (!pq.empty()) {                                              // step 3
        res.push_back(pq.top().second);
        pq.pop();
    }
    std::reverse(res.begin(), res.end());                              // step 4
    return res;
}
```

a) **(4 points)** Give the tight **average-case** running time of each of steps 1-4 in terms of n, d and k.

b) **(2 points)** Give the tight average-case running time of the whole function.

c) **(2 points)** Give the tight **worst-case** running time of the whole function and say which step is responsible.

d) **(2 points)** If `std::map` replaced `std::unordered_map`, what would the (worst-case) running time of the whole function be?

**Question 8 (coding, 13 points)**

Write a function that counts the **contiguous, non-empty subarrays** of `a` whose elements add up to exactly `k`. The array may contain negative numbers. For example, with `a = {3, 4, 7, 2, -3, 1, 4, 2}` and `k = 7` the answer is 4 (`{3,4}`, `{7}`, `{7,2,-3,1}`, `{1,4,2}`).

The function must run in **O(n) average time**, so you may not examine every pair of positions. Use `std::unordered_map` (hint: prefix sums).

```cpp
int countSubarraysWithSum(const std::vector<int>& a, int k) {
    // write code here
}
```

(10 points for the code.) Then **(3 points)**: give the average-case and worst-case running time and the extra space, with a brief justification.

questions.answer:
```cpp
int countSubarraysWithSum(const std::vector<int>& a, int k) {
    std::unordered_map<long long, int> seen;   // prefix sum -> how many prefixes have that sum
    seen[0] = 1;                               // the empty prefix
    long long prefix = 0;
    int count = 0;
    for (int x : a) {
        prefix += x;
        auto it = seen.find(prefix - k);       // a subarray ending here has sum k iff an earlier prefix = prefix - k
        if (it != seen.end())
            count += it->second;
        ++seen[prefix];                        // record AFTER counting so the subarray is non-empty
    }
    return count;
}
```

**Why it works.** Let P(j) be the sum of the first j elements. The subarray from position i to j-1 has sum P(j) - P(i), so it equals k exactly when P(i) = P(j) - k. For each new prefix, the number of earlier prefixes with value `prefix - k` is the number of subarrays ending here with sum k. The map is seeded with {0 : 1} so subarrays starting at position 0 are found, and the current prefix is added *after* the lookup so only earlier prefixes are used (non-empty subarrays).

**Complexity.** One pass of n iterations, each with one `find` and one `operator[]`, O(1) average: **O(n) average**; worst case all keys collide, each hash operation is O(n): **O(n^2)**. Extra space **O(n)** for up to n + 1 distinct prefix sums.

