questions.answer:
```cpp
class ClosestFinder {
public:
    void add(int x) { values.insert(x); }                  // O(log n)

    int closest(int q) const {                             // precondition: at least one value stored
        auto hi = values.lower_bound(q);                   // first stored value >= q, O(log n)
        if (hi == values.begin())                          // nothing smaller than q
            return *hi;
        auto lo = std::prev(hi);                           // largest stored value < q
        if (hi == values.end())                            // nothing >= q
            return *lo;
        long long dLo = (long long)q - *lo;
        long long dHi = (long long)*hi - q;
        return (dLo <= dHi) ? *lo : *hi;                   // tie -> the smaller value
    }

private:
    std::set<int> values;
};
```

**Complexity.** `add`: `set::insert` is O(log n) (balanced BST). `closest`: the member function `values.lower_bound(q)` descends the tree in O(log n); `std::prev` and the dereferences are O(1). The call must be the *member* `set::lower_bound`; the generic `std::lower_bound(values.begin(), values.end(), q)` would take O(n) on set iterators (they are not random access). A hash set stores keys by hash value, so it keeps no order: finding the nearest value would require scanning all n elements, O(n).
